Generic APIs in practice.

You can now read and write generics; this closing section is about judgement — when to make something generic, how to shape its signature, and how to read the dense generic APIs you meet daily. The goal isn't maximum genericity; it's an API that's flexible for callers, and honest about its contract.

So, when should you reach for generics? Make something generic when it does the same thing regardless of the element type — a container, an algorithm, a transformation. A box of T, a repository, a cache of K and V, a first-or-null utility. The logic genuinely doesn't care about the type, only the relationships between the types. And when should you not? Don't genericise when the type does matter, or when there's only ever one. A type parameter used exactly once, with no relationship to any other type or to the return, adds noise without safety — a plain type, or an interface, is clearer. The tell for a useful type parameter is that it appears at least twice in the signature, tying inputs to each other, or to the output.

Then, shape the signature with the two design tools from this module, which combine into a checklist. Bound a parameter when the method must call methods on T — T extends comparable to compare, T extends closeable to close. And apply P-E-C-S to each parameter — question-mark-extends-T for the ones you read from, question-mark-super-T for the ones you write to, and an exact T when you do both. Watch a real signature fall out of these rules. Copy, taking a destination of question-mark-super-T and a source of question-mark-extends-T, is pure P-E-C-S. And max — for a T comparable with itself, or a supertype, taking a collection producing T's, returning the largest — is bound plus P-E-C-S together. Once you know the vocabulary, max reads fluently. And that question-mark-super on comparable even lets a comparable-of-object order your T's — maximum flexibility, still safe.

And notice: most of the standard library's power is generic APIs, which you now have the vocabulary to read. Optional of T, a typed "maybe a value", ending null-returns — that's module seven. Comparator and comparable, orderings wired through question-mark-super-T — module four. The functional interfaces — function, predicate, supplier, consumer — the backbone of lambdas and streams, in modules six and seven, all generic. And stream, list, and map, which you've used throughout. Every one is "written once, works for any type", and their signatures are just bounds and P-E-C-S applied consistently.

So here are the principles to carry forward. Program to generic interfaces — accept a list of T, return an optional of T, and let callers pick the type. Be liberal in, specific out — use wildcards on your parameters, to accept the widest input, but return concrete types, like a list of T, not a list of question-mark-extends-T, so callers aren't stuck with a read-only result. Let inference work — declare the type argument once, on the variable or via the diamond, and let the compiler carry it. And push errors to compile time — that's the entire reason generics exist. A signature that encodes the type relationships turns a class of run-time failures into red squiggles in your editor.

That's generics, end to end: a compile-time system for expressing "this works for any type, and here's exactly how the types relate", checked before your program ever runs. Module six turns to another pillar of modern Java — treating functions themselves as values — and you'll find its function and predicate types are generics you can now read at a glance.
